Specific comments on what is readable and what is not:
What I noticed was readable:
    PlayerCollision is easy and straightforward to follow because HandleContact uses a switch statement to separate the different types of contact, including doors and enemies. HandleEnemyContact also keeps enemy behavior separate, making it easy to understand what happens when the player touches an enemy.
    Physics.cs is relatively simple. The methods Jump, Launch, SetGravity, and Apply all have a clear purpose, and keeping gravity and vertical velocity together makes it fairly easy to understand how the player's vertical movement is handled.
    BlockCollision uses many descriptive method names such as IsOnGround, TryJump, HasGroundAhead, and ResolveMovingBlockMovement. These names make it easier to locate specific collision operations.


    What I noticed could be improved:
    BlockCollision is quite large and contains several different responsibilities, including player movement, enemy ledge detection, moving platform movement, hazard contact, and projectile impacts. Although the individual methods are fairly  easy to understand, it can be difficult to identify where a particular type of collision behavior belongs.
    UpdateMovement is one of the longer methods and handles several operations in sequence, like moving platforms, horizontal movement, gravity, vertical collision, and ground-state updates. Splitting some of these operations into smaller helper methods could make the overall process easier to follow.
    Some methods in BlockCollision have similar overlapping responsibilities, such as UpdateVerticalMovement and UpdateMovement. It could be helpful to make their specific uses clearer so that someone working on the collision system can better understand when each method should be called.



CODE QUALITY REVIEW

