Specific comments on code quality:
What I noticed:
    The separation between Physics, BlockCollision, and PlayerCollision is good because each class handles a different part of the movement and collision system. Physics handles gravity and velocity, BlockCollision resolves movement against level geometry, and PlayerCollision handles interactions between the player and game objects.
    BlockCollision is reusable across game objects because of its use of IGameObject and the ConditionalWeakTable that stores collision states. This means different objects can  use the same collision system while maintaining their own supporting blocks and ground state.
    PlayerCollision keeps contact behavior outside of the player's movement logic. It also checks whether an enemy has been destroyed before handling contact, so destroyed enemies can not continue to damage the player.
    Physics is a centralized way to apply gravity and launch objects. SetGravity also resets vertical velocity when gravity changes, which helps prevent existing movement from carrying over when the object's physics settings are updated.


Hypothetical change:
  If we wanted to add a new type of moving platform interaction in a future sprint, I think the existing BlockCollision system would need to be altered a bit. ResolveMovingBlockMovement currently handles both moving platforms that push an object and platforms that the player is standing on. As we continue to add more platform behaviors, it might become difficult to maintain all of these interactions within one method.
  I would consider further separating the different moving platform interactions into smaller methods, like handling platforms that carry the player and platforms that collide with or push the player. This would make it easier to add new platform behaviors without making the main movement update very complicated. I would also test these interactions with jumping, knockback, and falling to make sure that the player's position and supporting platform state remain consistent. We have faced collision bugs in the past so we have to be careful moving forward.
